{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151286","version_id":"a393edb1af792a0f22f58a9c9be7640474dbeb4ed41d7ec336f5428b607cf2bc","decision":"defer","reason":"The item is relevant to CAD and PLM workflows and is fresh, but its substantive claims are Autodesk executives' forward-looking product and adoption claims rather than results checked under stated conditions. The agentic PLM is described as launching in coming quarters, while claims of leading automation and strong adoption have no disclosed task set, attempt count, or independent evaluation. A released feature with reproducible workflow evidence, or customer-side verification of data extraction and PLM actions, would make it checkable.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151329","version_id":"a5a6e64fd4f2a87b5b45be5afd0c01d19fd3ee1e8b8f12be9e7e49964e03d82b","decision":"defer","reason":"This is fresh and relevant CAD/simulation coverage, but the MCP and SimScale-agent capabilities are principally vendor announcements, with no disclosed success rate, model conditions, simulation-ready-geometry criteria, or evidence that the generated setups are valid. Engineering.com’s report and the linked SimScale release are not independent corroboration of the same underlying claim. A released integration tested on named CAD models against engineer-reviewed boundary conditions and meshes would resolve the missing evidence.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.engineering.com/?p=151563","version_id":"90e3adc0a87a45dc20689ae62db7125fe378f8191522f5954fae4ace5e96c803","decision":"accept","reason":"This is fresh, highly relevant CAD evidence because the author performed a stated check: imported two drawing-to-CAD STEP outputs into Fusion, assigned the specified material, and verified each target mass. It also reports concrete failure modes—a partial Fusion feature-tree import and failure on a seven-body shelf—so the evidence supports exact solid/mass results on two selected practice drawings, not general manufacturability or reliable parametric reconstruction. The perspective is independent hands-on testing, although broader replication and production-drawing coverage remain missing.","piece_ids":["01","02","03"]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/ai-generated-thumbnail-description-search","version_id":"b2755a997c3cdda71421bbdd40ee9721d353cbc20ed2f1f80e91169757b9bbd2","decision":"accept","reason":"The vendor post provides a checkable, current product-scope claim: the opt-in Labs feature generates searchable text from static thumbnails and is limited to public documents. It is relevant to CAD reuse and data discovery, but the claimed reductions in duplicate designs and time spent searching are speculative; no retrieval-quality, false-match, or customer-workflow evaluation is supplied. This commercial perspective should be paired with practitioner evidence, especially before extending conclusions to private or supplier data.","piece_ids":["01","03"]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.onshape.com/en/blog/featurescript-mcp-server-use-cases-text-code-cad","version_id":"9656a0a35f95ac0e0adb6ba1530e2759342633cbcecba8de9948699a032ab5a8","decision":"defer","reason":"The FeatureScript MCP server is relevant to text-to-code-to-CAD, but this vendor article mostly presents possible use cases and illustrative outcomes rather than a checked example. Assertions that an LLM can preserve parametric intent, manufacturing accuracy, or consolidate complex models have no supplied code, model settings, validation criteria, or failure rate. A reproducible FeatureScript example with geometric, standards, and regression checks would make the claims usable.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"podcast:173f6c2b-e1b8-4de0-b7b7-4b15658d52e6","version_id":"da6be0ed10587e503de7706e1ad94cd3d0409d0c4664a004a631d6f27c500a73","decision":"reject","reason":"Although fresh and timestamped, this discussion concerns narrative intelligence, coaching, and human roles in general AI rather than 3D modeling, CAD, geometry, simulation-ready assets, or physical-system verification. The supplied passages offer personal interpretation rather than a relevant capability check. It therefore fails the publication’s relevance criterion.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"podcast:509842f7-f592-432c-b01a-ad26a2ae1e31","version_id":"122e3cc3de2a41fbd9f0de08a089614db628a34697f00a720e1d3abf5b405dca","decision":"reject","reason":"This is a fresh, timestamped discussion of education and AI, but it does not bear on CAD, 3D modeling, geometry, simulation, or intelligent systems acting in physical space. Its observations are host perspectives rather than evidence from a relevant engineering workflow. It fails relevance despite mentioning mechanical engineering in passing.","piece_ids":[]}
{"item_id":"podcast:b836fa1b-9246-43d5-8cbb-cf1446760a6d","version_id":"a0beee0f49195b6f0b08b75af793b6f6acaf56a159b58771b1c5d8f6c294b282","decision":"accept","reason":"This fresh, timestamped NVIDIA interview is relevant as supporting physical-AI context: at 00:11:45–00:12:48, the guest explicitly reports that differing robot sensors make a universal model difficult, and at 00:23:52–00:24:19 describes world simulation as conditioning future outputs on observations and action sequences. Those are attributable explanations of the speaker's view, not independently verified capability or deployment claims; NVIDIA’s commercial interest and the automatic transcript limit stronger conclusions. It adds a vendor perspective, while customer and working-engineer evidence remains absent.","piece_ids":["02","03"]}
{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/008","version_id":"1cb4eefeb597341d29e650ad7c122c9eea10b1aa146aef9c4a6214acdf2c2983","decision":"accept","reason":"This fresh robotics item is relevant as supporting evidence about how physical-AI claims are checked rather than as proof of CAD capability. At 00:03:29–00:04:32, the host reports a blind evaluation on a Franka arm with ten tasks, three attempts, common timing and scoring, and video; at 00:02:10–00:02:20, the host correctly limits a simulation leaderboard’s meaning for a real arm. The reported scores remain unverified because this sampled automatic transcript is not an independent audit, and the evaluation was commissioned by the model developer, but the timestamped account can support a narrow attributed discussion of evaluation design.","piece_ids":["02","03"]}
{"item_id":"podcast:https://physicalaipodcast.co/episodes/009","version_id":"13aaf6bfc525bce0b9a1887162b0c0c3d4dd024b9bafd1c223a5bc48bd063692","decision":"accept","reason":"This is fresh supporting robotics context with a concrete, timestamped scrutiny claim: at 00:05:24–00:05:50, the host reports a held-out-task comparison in which explicit and latent world-action models differ sharply, and at 00:07:21–00:07:35 names the limits—mostly simulation, one real-robot task, and a five-day-old paper—while noting released code and weights. The numerical results are still only podcast-reported and automatic-transcript figures, so they should be attributed until the underlying paper and evaluation are checked. Its value is the evidence tension between in-distribution speed claims and out-of-distribution physical performance, not proof of deployed robot reliability.","piece_ids":["02","03"]}
